  "kind": "trig_identity",
  "title": "A double-angle identity",
  "prompt": "Prove that $\\dfrac{1 - \\cos 2x}{\\sin 2x} = \\tan x$.",
  "answer_format": "Steps only: start from one side, end at the other. No final answer needed.",
  "difficulty": 2,
  "tags": [
    "identities",
    "double angle"
  ],
  "data": {
    "lhs": "(1 - cos(2x))/sin(2x)",
    "rhs": "tan(x)"
  },
  "answer": "",
  "mistakes": [],
  "hints": [
    "Rewrite cos 2x with the version of the double-angle formula that cancels the 1.",
    "sin 2x = 2 sin x cos x."
  ],
  "solution": [
    "$\\cos 2x = 1 - 2\\sin^2 x$, so the numerator is $2\\sin^2 x$.",
    "$\\sin 2x = 2\\sin x\\cos x$, so the left side is $\\frac{2\\sin^2 x}{2\\sin x\\cos x} = \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} = \\tan x$."
  ],
  "reference": {
    "book": "openstax-precalc2e",
    "section": "7.3 Double-Angle, Half-Angle, and Reduction Formulas",
    "note": "Original problem."
  },
